WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:04.640
Alright, let's begin lecture 21 on Gaussian integers and primes.

00:05.640 --> 00:09.720
So, first of all, the prime divisor property for the Gaussian integers.

00:09.840 --> 00:14.560
I mentioned this in the last lecture, but let's look at the proof in this one.

00:15.320 --> 00:19.140
So, a Gaussian prime omega divides alpha beta,

00:19.480 --> 00:23.960
then that means either omega divides alpha or omega divides beta.

00:24.300 --> 00:24.880
Here's the proof.

00:25.680 --> 00:28.760
So, suppose omega is a Gaussian prime,

00:28.860 --> 00:32.920
and omega divides alpha, the product of alpha and beta, in the Gaussian integers.

00:33.860 --> 00:36.020
Alright, so suppose it doesn't divide alpha.

00:36.020 --> 00:38.260
Then it remains to show that alpha divides beta.

00:39.480 --> 00:43.480
Alright, so Bazou's theorem says that there exist x and y Gaussian integers

00:43.480 --> 00:48.220
for which alpha x plus omega y is the GCD of alpha omega.

00:48.640 --> 00:48.900
Alright?

00:48.900 --> 00:56.240
And if we multiply by beta, that gives us alpha beta x plus omega beta y equals to beta times the GCD.

00:57.000 --> 01:02.080
Note that there exists a gamma such that alpha beta equals the gamma w

01:02.080 --> 01:06.620
because we're assuming that omega divides the product of alpha and beta.

01:06.620 --> 01:14.540
Alright, therefore, we have that the GCD of alpha and omega times beta

01:14.540 --> 01:21.180
is equal to the product of omega with the quantity x gamma plus y beta.

01:21.180 --> 01:23.180
Hey, but that means that omega...

01:23.180 --> 01:25.460
Well, what else?

01:25.600 --> 01:25.980
Let's see here.

01:27.620 --> 01:34.900
And more than that, since omega does not divide alpha and omega is a Gaussian prime,

01:35.620 --> 01:40.220
that means that the GCD of alpha and omega can be shown to be a unit.

01:40.720 --> 01:44.140
That would make a good homework exercise, which I guess I didn't assign.

01:44.140 --> 01:54.580
But, um, thus beta is equal to some unit times x gamma plus y beta parentheses times times omega.

01:55.800 --> 02:00.460
Um, so that shows that, of course, omega divides beta, and that completes the proof.

02:00.800 --> 02:06.420
Now, if you take a good look at this proof, you'll figure out that this is exactly the same proof

02:06.420 --> 02:07.420
we gave for the integers.

02:08.160 --> 02:11.220
It's just now we're using Bazou's theorem and the Gaussian integers.

02:11.220 --> 02:15.580
But really, um, the argument is pretty much identical.

02:16.160 --> 02:20.920
And that's the case for a lot of things, um, where arithmetic transfers from the integers

02:20.920 --> 02:22.520
to the Gaussian integers many times.

02:23.100 --> 02:25.420
Essentially, the same proof transfers through.

02:25.720 --> 02:32.040
Like, all of the basic theorems about divisibility, um, you know, like if a divides b and a divides c,

02:32.160 --> 02:36.860
then a divides, um, b plus c, those kind of divisibility theorems,

02:36.940 --> 02:39.800
they're still true, um, certainly for the Gaussian integers.

02:39.800 --> 02:44.760
Now, here's something I didn't quite say in the last lecture, but I probably should have.

02:44.860 --> 02:46.360
But anyway, it's just as well I'm saying it now.

02:47.220 --> 02:52.140
So, if you have an odd positive integer p, right,

02:52.380 --> 02:59.580
that means that either it's in 4z plus 1 or it's in 4z plus 3 as sets.

02:59.580 --> 03:05.280
Um, the other things, like 4z or 4z plus 2, those are even integers, right?

03:05.380 --> 03:11.260
So, every odd integer is either in the, um, 4z plus 1 class or the 4z plus 3 class.

03:12.080 --> 03:16.940
And, um, if you think about the quadratic form, if you think about a squared plus b squared, right,

03:17.560 --> 03:23.700
and you think about the values that that could take on as it cycles through either things from here or from there,

03:23.700 --> 03:27.860
well, you can pretty quickly figure out that you don't get 3.

03:28.640 --> 03:35.900
Um, so if you look at x squared and z4, you take 0 squared, you get 0, 1 squared, you get 1, 2 squared, you get 0,

03:36.400 --> 03:38.920
3 squared, you get 9, which is 1, and z4.

03:38.920 --> 03:45.220
So, if we look at the square of values, mod 4, we're either going to get 0 or 1.

03:45.640 --> 03:51.780
So, when you take the sum of squares, mod 4, you can't get 3, because that would take either 2 plus 1 or a,

03:52.260 --> 03:57.400
you know, it would take a 2 plus 1, and we just don't have the 2 as an output for the square, right?

03:58.140 --> 04:01.240
Um, so we can get 0 and 1 and 1 and 0.

04:01.360 --> 04:06.680
So, we can get x squared plus y squared equals to 1, mod 4, but we can't get it equal to 3, mod 4.

04:06.680 --> 04:16.600
So, that means that no, um, odd integer of the form 4z plus 3 can be written directly as a sum of squares like that.

04:16.760 --> 04:18.400
You know, a lot of integer squares, at least.

04:19.140 --> 04:23.620
Now, this is something we have said in, actually, more than one lecture.

04:23.740 --> 04:26.060
This was first, I think, mentioned in lecture 4.

04:26.920 --> 04:33.000
But, man, it may have been a lecture, I forget which, there was a later lecture where we did that explicitly once we had modular arithmetic.

04:33.840 --> 04:34.780
Alright, so that's that.

04:34.780 --> 04:41.500
So, now let's move on here to, we'll be using that, um, prime divisor property as we go on.

04:41.680 --> 04:43.360
So, let's get to it.

04:46.080 --> 04:55.420
Um, if a plus ib with a b non-zero is a Gaussian prime, then a squared plus b squared is prime in the natural numbers.

04:56.380 --> 04:58.420
Ah, okay, so let's just prove it.

04:58.420 --> 05:04.960
If a plus ib is a Gaussian prime, then we saw last lecture that a minus ib is also a Gaussian prime.

05:05.980 --> 05:13.500
But, um, we can multiply a plus ib times a minus b and get a squared plus b squared.

05:14.280 --> 05:14.520
Right?

05:14.900 --> 05:17.520
And if we set that equals to p, well, that's a natural number.

05:18.500 --> 05:19.860
And let's prove it's prime.

05:20.220 --> 05:22.120
So, suppose it had a factorization.

05:23.120 --> 05:26.200
r, s, where r and s were, you know, non-unit factors.

05:27.200 --> 05:33.540
Then, um, if we multiply a plus ib times a minus ib, it would also be equal to r times s.

05:33.960 --> 05:34.820
Let's call that star.

05:35.100 --> 05:35.400
All right?

05:36.500 --> 05:39.580
But there's a prime factorization theorem for Gaussian integers, remember?

05:40.360 --> 05:42.940
And so, I think that that's going to get us into trouble here, see?

05:42.940 --> 05:51.680
Because if, um, if r and s, um, are, if they can't be factored, if r and s are Gaussian primes, then we're in trouble.

05:52.300 --> 05:59.860
Because we'd have a plus ib and a minus ib having to match up somehow with r and s upon multiplication by a unit.

05:59.860 --> 06:05.380
But multiplying a plus ib or a minus ib by a unit doesn't switch it to be a real Gaussian prime.

06:05.540 --> 06:07.260
That's, that's not, they're not the same type.

06:08.160 --> 06:13.940
Um, and on the other hand, if r, s is a product of Gaussian prime factors, you're still in trouble.

06:13.940 --> 06:21.340
Because then you've got two Gaussian prime factors on this side, but then you've got, like, four on the other, or more.

06:21.920 --> 06:28.300
Um, so, in other words, star indicates that p has inequivalent Gaussian prime factorizations,

06:28.300 --> 06:33.400
which contradicts the, um, fundamental theorem of arithmetic for Gaussian integers.

06:34.060 --> 06:38.740
Therefore, no such factorization of p exists, and we find p is a prime in the integers.

06:39.400 --> 06:39.720
Hooray!

06:40.340 --> 06:41.360
So that's kind of neat.

06:42.960 --> 06:43.600
All right.

06:45.120 --> 06:54.520
So, every Gaussian prime, every complex Gaussian prime, gives us a corresponding real prime of the form a squared plus b squared, right?

06:54.520 --> 06:59.960
So, it must be that that prime is in 4n plus 1 from what we said on page 1 of this lecture, right?

07:01.400 --> 07:02.520
4z plus 1, rather.

07:03.180 --> 07:03.960
All right, going on.

07:05.880 --> 07:13.460
All right, so now we need to look at a lemma, um, due to Lagrange, um, in preparation for, uh, Fermat's two-square theorem.

07:13.760 --> 07:14.060
All right.

07:14.640 --> 07:19.560
Now, Fermat stated the theorem, but Lagrange, um, you know, proved it later.

07:19.560 --> 07:23.560
And, um, I'm not, I'm a little fuzzy on the history.

07:23.680 --> 07:31.720
He may have been a person who proved it before Lagrange as well, but, um, it's probably fair to say Lagrange improved a lot of existing arguments, if nothing else.

07:32.480 --> 07:37.500
Um, although this may, well, anyway, I'm not sure about the history, so let me just not say anything.

07:38.500 --> 07:41.300
Um, but this is Lagrange's lemma, anyway.

07:41.300 --> 07:49.380
Uh, prime, um, p equals 4 to the m plus 1 divides m squared plus 1 for some m in the integers.

07:49.680 --> 07:50.940
All right, so let's see how that's proved.

07:51.020 --> 07:51.520
It's kind of neat.

07:52.420 --> 07:59.060
So, one of the things we proved in lecture 13, which you may have forgotten about, is Wilson's theorem.

07:59.280 --> 08:05.520
Wilson's theorem, among other things, said that if you have a prime p, and you look at p minus 1 factorial,

08:06.200 --> 08:09.120
that's congruent to minus 1 mod p.

08:09.120 --> 08:13.320
It's actually an if and only a theorem, but we're only really using one direction of it, I suppose.

08:14.160 --> 08:19.000
Um, so anyway, p minus 1 factorial is congruent to minus 1 mod p.

08:19.300 --> 08:25.400
Now, if the p is equal to 4n plus 1, then 4n plus 1 minus 1 is just 4n.

08:25.540 --> 08:30.940
So what we get is minus 1 is congruent to 4n factorial mod p, right?

08:31.640 --> 08:35.500
But then, that's, of course, 1 times 2 times 3 times da-da-da times 4n,

08:35.500 --> 08:39.320
which we could break up as 1 times 2 times da-da-da all the way to 2n,

08:39.360 --> 08:41.840
and then 2n plus 1 all the way to 4n.

08:41.840 --> 08:47.420
But then, since we're working mod p, and p is equal to, you know,

08:48.820 --> 08:55.560
remember, p is equal to, what was it, 4n plus 1.

08:57.560 --> 09:03.220
So that means that, like, you know, um, and that's congruent to 0.

09:03.220 --> 09:10.840
So that means that 2n plus 1 is congruent to minus 2n, etc.

09:12.680 --> 09:13.120
Right?

09:13.420 --> 09:20.240
So I can trade that 2n plus 1 for a minus 2n on down the line

09:20.240 --> 09:22.420
until I get the 4n and trade it to a minus 1.

09:23.000 --> 09:23.980
So what do you got?

09:24.060 --> 09:29.480
Well, you've got the product of 1 to 2n squared times the minus 1 to the 2n factor,

09:29.480 --> 09:30.500
which, of course, is just 1.

09:31.140 --> 09:35.460
So we get 2n factorial squared is congruent to minus 1 mod p.

09:35.980 --> 09:36.940
But, hey, that's what we wanted.

09:37.060 --> 09:40.120
We wanted to show that there exists an m, in this case, 2n factorial,

09:40.700 --> 09:43.160
whose square plus 1 is congruent to 0 mod p,

09:43.540 --> 09:46.000
which is to say that p divides m squared plus 1.

09:46.600 --> 09:48.480
So, hey, that's pretty good.

09:49.680 --> 09:52.580
All right, so this is Lagrange's lemma.

09:52.580 --> 10:01.100
Now, so that brings us to Fermat's theorem on the sum of two squares.

10:02.680 --> 10:06.480
As is the case with many things involving Fermat, he's stated it,

10:06.560 --> 10:11.180
but he, I think this is, it's maybe one of those things he proved,

10:11.400 --> 10:12.860
he claimed to prove by dissent.

10:14.640 --> 10:15.560
I'm trying to remember.

10:15.560 --> 10:21.500
I don't know, but I think it's fair to say that the careful proof of these things

10:21.500 --> 10:23.180
was left to later mathematicians.

10:24.060 --> 10:30.340
But, you know, Fermat's, the early 1600s, 1630, 50, somewhere in there, I think.

10:30.760 --> 10:33.420
And, you know, anyway.

10:34.440 --> 10:35.240
So here it is.

10:35.340 --> 10:39.140
An odd prime p can be expressed as the sum of squares,

10:40.000 --> 10:41.380
sum of squares of integers,

10:42.040 --> 10:44.660
if and only if p is congruent to 1 mod 4.

10:44.660 --> 10:45.560
All right.

10:46.540 --> 10:47.820
So let's sort through it.

10:48.440 --> 10:51.000
So I start with the converse direction, actually.

10:51.100 --> 10:52.960
I assume p is congruent to 1 mod 4.

10:53.720 --> 10:57.700
Then by Lagrange's lemma, there exists an m for which p divides m squared plus 1.

10:58.020 --> 10:58.380
All right.

10:59.040 --> 11:02.560
But that means that p divides the product of m plus i and m minus i.

11:03.480 --> 11:06.520
However, if p divides either one of those things, right,

11:06.540 --> 11:08.400
if p divides m plus or minus i,

11:08.880 --> 11:12.140
then that means that m plus or minus i is equal to pq

11:12.140 --> 11:15.620
for some q in the Gaussian integers.

11:15.960 --> 11:22.520
But that would then mean that m plus or minus i divided by p is a Gaussian integer.

11:22.660 --> 11:24.940
But that's just simply not the case.

11:25.300 --> 11:27.960
And the way we can see that is this plus i piece.

11:28.560 --> 11:31.520
See, plus i over p, that would be 1 over p,

11:31.600 --> 11:33.060
which is clearly not an integer.

11:33.480 --> 11:35.760
The coefficient of i here is clearly not an integer,

11:35.760 --> 11:37.760
so that's clearly not a Gaussian integer,

11:38.280 --> 11:41.260
which, of course, contradicts the assumption, right?

11:42.600 --> 11:47.260
Which means, then, that p does not divide either m plus i or m minus i.

11:47.580 --> 11:51.840
But that's a problem because we have the prime divisor property, right?

11:53.220 --> 11:55.940
So, in other words, well, it's not a problem yet.

11:56.020 --> 11:58.380
But what that means is p is not a Gaussian prime, right?

11:58.380 --> 12:00.960
Because it doesn't satisfy the prime divisor property,

12:00.960 --> 12:02.820
which every Gaussian prime must do,

12:02.960 --> 12:05.100
according to the thing we proved on page 1.

12:06.240 --> 12:06.840
So then what?

12:07.320 --> 12:07.640
All right.

12:07.840 --> 12:11.060
Well, therefore, p is an ordinary prime of the form a squared plus b squared.

12:11.940 --> 12:14.120
And there's a theorem.

12:14.740 --> 12:15.580
How do I know that?

12:15.660 --> 12:19.060
Well, because we had a theorem from page 6 of lecture 20,

12:19.140 --> 12:20.900
which said that p is a natural number,

12:21.460 --> 12:22.180
p is a Gauss prime,

12:22.280 --> 12:24.640
if and only if there does not exist an a and b,

12:24.740 --> 12:26.660
such that p is equal to a squared plus b squared, right?

12:26.660 --> 12:31.480
So, we just showed that p is not a Gaussian prime,

12:31.600 --> 12:33.560
which means that there does exist a and b,

12:33.620 --> 12:35.460
such that p is equal to a squared plus b squared.

12:35.620 --> 12:36.820
So, there you have it.

12:37.480 --> 12:39.800
Now, conversely, or rather, in the forward direction of the proof,

12:40.340 --> 12:43.000
if p is not a prime and p is equal to a squared plus b squared

12:43.000 --> 12:44.060
for some a and b and z,

12:44.640 --> 12:47.480
then as p is not congruent to 3 mod 4,

12:47.580 --> 12:49.260
we have p is congruent to 1 mod 4

12:49.260 --> 12:51.340
by the remark at the start of this lecture.

12:51.340 --> 12:58.840
So, that's, you know, relatively straightforward.

12:59.800 --> 13:02.040
So, that's Fermat's theorem on the sum of squares.

13:02.200 --> 13:06.740
Now, there is, you know, a slightly better theorem,

13:07.280 --> 13:09.420
which is just on the sum of squares.

13:09.640 --> 13:12.220
I don't have a proof of this theorem for this lecture,

13:12.380 --> 13:16.260
but I think if you really take a hard look at everything we've done,

13:16.380 --> 13:18.880
you could piece together the proof for this theorem

13:18.880 --> 13:20.140
without too much trouble.

13:20.140 --> 13:23.060
An integer n greater than 1

13:23.060 --> 13:25.560
can be written as the sum of squares

13:25.560 --> 13:28.040
for some integers a and b

13:28.040 --> 13:32.480
if and only if the prime decomposition of that integer n, right,

13:32.580 --> 13:35.300
contains no factor p to the power k,

13:35.440 --> 13:38.340
where p is a prime congruent to 3 mod 4,

13:38.480 --> 13:41.880
and that prime power k is odd.

13:42.880 --> 13:43.260
All right?

13:44.560 --> 13:47.940
So, let's see why that makes sense.

13:47.940 --> 13:49.240
All right, so with some examples.

13:50.140 --> 13:52.200
So, 15.

13:52.460 --> 13:54.400
Can we write 15 as the sum of squares?

13:56.400 --> 13:57.720
No conduzville, right?

13:57.840 --> 14:01.600
So, 3 times 5 is the 15.

14:02.100 --> 14:03.100
I factor the 3 out.

14:03.220 --> 14:05.500
Now, 5 is of the form 4n plus 1.

14:06.020 --> 14:08.020
So, of course, we can find a sum of squares,

14:08.080 --> 14:10.640
which gives us back 5, namely 1 squared plus 2 squared.

14:10.640 --> 14:15.060
But there's no way I can factor 3 into this

14:15.060 --> 14:18.860
and get it as to something squared plus something squared, right?

14:18.920 --> 14:19.460
So, no.

14:19.860 --> 14:24.220
15 is not in S2 in the language we introduced last lecture, right?

14:24.220 --> 14:29.380
So, I mean, this essentially tells you...

14:29.380 --> 14:30.840
This basically says what?

14:30.920 --> 14:35.180
This basically, you know, explains...

14:35.180 --> 14:47.160
Which n are in S2, right?

14:48.160 --> 14:51.420
So, it answers the question posed in the last lecture.

14:51.580 --> 14:52.080
Like, what...

14:52.080 --> 14:54.000
Which integers are sums of squares?

14:54.140 --> 14:57.380
Well, the ones that meet this criteria, right?

14:57.380 --> 14:59.380
Um...

14:59.380 --> 15:01.380
Okay, so...

15:02.500 --> 15:03.380
Now...

15:05.060 --> 15:05.380
Um...

15:06.260 --> 15:07.380
Do-do-do-do-do-do...

15:07.380 --> 15:08.540
45.

15:08.780 --> 15:13.140
Well, 45 has got a 3 in it, but the 3 is squared, right?

15:15.200 --> 15:17.680
So, 3, of course, is congruent to 3 mod 4, right?

15:17.960 --> 15:19.760
But the problem here was it was an odd power.

15:20.200 --> 15:21.400
This time it's an even power.

15:21.540 --> 15:25.440
And you see what I can do then is I can bring the 3 into both of these

15:25.440 --> 15:27.600
and I get 3 times 1 squared, which is 3 squared.

15:27.720 --> 15:31.340
And 3 times 2 squared, which is 6 squared.

15:32.040 --> 15:34.500
And so, 45 is 3 squared plus 6 squared.

15:34.580 --> 15:35.840
It's 9 plus 36.

15:35.980 --> 15:36.980
Of course, that's true, right?

15:37.620 --> 15:38.060
Pretty cool.

15:39.480 --> 15:40.200
Um...

15:40.200 --> 15:40.920
How about 30?

15:40.980 --> 15:42.540
Can we write 30 as the sum of squares?

15:44.680 --> 15:45.200
Um...

15:45.200 --> 15:47.080
Well, that's 2 times 3 times 5.

15:47.420 --> 15:51.120
So, we've just got one copy of the prime that's congruent to 3 mod 4.

15:51.280 --> 15:51.800
So, no.

15:52.020 --> 15:52.560
Can't do it.

15:52.640 --> 15:53.180
Can't do it.

15:53.880 --> 15:54.240
Alright?

15:55.440 --> 15:57.140
Let's try another one.

15:57.180 --> 15:58.480
Here's a bigger example.

16:00.600 --> 16:03.340
Bigger, bigger, bigger example.

16:03.920 --> 16:05.640
9 times 5 times 41.

16:06.640 --> 16:07.080
Alright?

16:07.260 --> 16:11.040
So, that's 9 times 2 plus i times 2 minus i.

16:11.720 --> 16:15.640
And so here, rather than just directly, um...

16:15.640 --> 16:19.140
You know, writing 5 as the sum of squares and 41 as the sum of squares,

16:19.220 --> 16:20.140
which we could, of course, do,

16:20.520 --> 16:24.060
it's more fun to write them in terms of their complex factors.

16:24.060 --> 16:32.120
Because if I do that, then I can combine them and figure out, you know, what I get in total here.

16:32.120 --> 16:37.840
So, what I do is I multiply the 1 plus 2i times the 4 plus 5, 5 plus 4i.

16:37.840 --> 16:41.640
And I multiply the conjugates of those to get the conjugate right.

16:43.440 --> 16:53.680
And, um, that gave me 3 plus 14i minus 3 minus 14i, which gives me 3 squared plus 14 squared, all times 9.

16:53.680 --> 16:59.160
But that 9 is a 3 squared, so I can bring it in and get 3 times 3 squared plus 3 times 14 squared,

16:59.240 --> 17:06.800
which gives me the somewhat non-obvious fact that 1845 is equal to 9 squared plus 42 squared.

17:07.060 --> 17:08.400
Um, did you know that?

17:08.400 --> 17:10.320
I mean, I didn't know it before with this lecture.

17:11.160 --> 17:15.660
Um, I would point out that another way you could go here,

17:17.040 --> 17:20.300
and, um, so here's another way we could have gone here.

17:20.940 --> 17:25.880
We could have done, we could have grouped, like, um,

17:25.880 --> 17:33.780
1 minus 2i with, um, 5 plus 4i.

17:36.060 --> 17:43.060
And then the other ones, 1 plus 2i and 5 minus 4i.

17:44.060 --> 17:46.880
If you look at it, it's still going to be,

17:48.100 --> 17:51.300
so this, this equals, only goes to here, okay.

17:51.940 --> 17:55.700
Um, if you look at it, it's still the product of a complex number and it's conjugate.

17:55.880 --> 17:56.100
Right?

17:56.860 --> 18:00.000
So, like, let me be careful here.

18:00.060 --> 18:05.640
5 and, um, plus 8 is 13, I believe.

18:06.860 --> 18:13.620
And I get minus 10i plus 4i, so I believe that is minus 6i.

18:14.620 --> 18:16.080
If I haven't made a mistake.

18:16.920 --> 18:18.660
Sadly, I have no calculator close by,

18:18.760 --> 18:22.380
so we'll just have to go on the hope that I didn't make a mistake.

18:22.500 --> 18:25.580
That's 5 plus 8, which is 13.

18:25.880 --> 18:30.440
10i minus 4i is plus 6i, right?

18:31.860 --> 18:35.300
So this, and there's still the 9.

18:36.040 --> 18:38.040
I forgot about the 9.

18:38.040 --> 18:45.620
So that's 9 times 13 squared plus 6 squared, right?

18:48.500 --> 18:52.260
Which is then bringing the 3 squared into both.

18:52.340 --> 18:54.580
We get 3 times 13, which is 39 squared.

18:54.580 --> 18:58.380
3 times 6 is 18 plus 18 squared.

19:00.040 --> 19:03.460
So I think I've given you at least one homework problem where I ask you to find

19:03.460 --> 19:08.300
distinct ways to write a given number as sums of squares.

19:08.300 --> 19:10.460
This is the game you can play.

19:10.460 --> 19:22.600
You can shift around the complex subfactors of the numbers and pair them differently to get different overall complex numbers being multiplied.

19:22.600 --> 19:32.820
It always, as long as you shift, like, as long as you pair, you know, you don't take both copies from the number.

19:32.820 --> 19:39.640
If you just take one of the, you know, the factors, one of the complex factors, one of the Gaussian prime factors of these primes,

19:39.640 --> 19:50.500
um, as long as you take one of those from each original integer factor, that will give you, um, a number and then times its conjugate.

19:52.080 --> 19:56.500
Well, anyway, I hope that hint helps you enough, but, uh, let's go on here.

20:00.340 --> 20:05.480
Another application of Gaussian integers is the study of Pythagorean triples.

20:05.480 --> 20:12.520
All right, so, I think we've talked about these a little bit before, although my mind fails me.

20:12.860 --> 20:14.080
Where did we talk about them?

20:14.840 --> 20:21.040
Um, anyway, lest my mind fail me again, the secret word for today is ace of clubs.

20:21.540 --> 20:22.020
There you go.

20:24.720 --> 20:33.100
And, um, so Pythagorean triple is, um, let's say, uh, three numbers, x, y, z integers,

20:33.100 --> 20:35.560
such that x squared plus y squared is equal to z squared.

20:36.220 --> 20:40.020
And, um, I'd like for them to all be non-zero to make it interesting.

20:40.960 --> 20:42.060
Um, all right.

20:42.880 --> 20:47.800
So, let's look at this equation in light of the Gaussian integers, right?

20:50.100 --> 20:54.360
Z squared is x squared plus y squared, but z squared is x plus i y times x minus i y.

20:54.360 --> 20:59.980
So, by the prime factorization, if the GCD of x plus i y and x minus i y is one,

21:00.620 --> 21:08.560
so I think that would be true, that would be true if, um, let's say we've reduced, um, x and y and z.

21:08.680 --> 21:15.700
So, any, like, common, if there's, like, a common factor of three in both x, y, and z, divide it out, right?

21:15.700 --> 21:21.320
Um, so, like, put x, y, and z in their lowest terms for this to be true.

21:22.660 --> 21:23.140
Excuse me.

21:23.260 --> 21:30.100
So, then, you can prove that x plus i y and x minus i y, um, are, are relatively prime in z i,

21:30.200 --> 21:33.680
provided that x and y are relatively prime over the integers.

21:35.460 --> 21:37.520
That seems like a good homework question I didn't ask.

21:38.160 --> 21:43.340
Um, I guess good homework questions I didn't ask make even better test questions, right?

21:43.340 --> 21:46.220
Heh, heh, heh, heh, yeah, all right.

21:47.320 --> 21:52.000
Um, then, x plus i y, all right, if this is equal to that, then it must be,

21:52.440 --> 21:55.980
you have a square on the left-hand side, so these must also be squares, all right?

21:56.460 --> 21:59.440
So, that means x plus i y should be a square in the Gaussian integers,

22:00.260 --> 22:02.560
but that, that means things.

22:03.060 --> 22:07.900
x plus, x minus i y also, so x minus i y is, let's say, u minus i v squared,

22:08.860 --> 22:13.060
and squaring that gives me this, equating the real part with the real part

22:13.060 --> 22:14.780
gives me x equals u squared minus v squared.

22:15.500 --> 22:19.220
Equating the imaginary part with the imaginary part gives me y is equal to 2u v,

22:20.080 --> 22:22.560
but, remember, z squared is x squared plus y squared,

22:22.640 --> 22:24.360
so that gives us z squared is equal to this,

22:24.980 --> 22:28.820
which, um, the cross term here is minus 2u squared v squared,

22:28.940 --> 22:31.260
but this one gives you plus 4u squared v squared,

22:31.360 --> 22:33.480
so a net of 2u squared v squared,

22:33.920 --> 22:36.900
which then is the square of u squared plus v squared squared.

22:36.900 --> 22:41.680
Hey, that's awesome, because then we can solve for z if we want the positive one,

22:42.220 --> 22:45.920
and I do, turn that frown the other way, all right?

22:46.360 --> 22:49.720
So, z is equal to u squared plus v squared.

22:50.300 --> 22:52.640
Aha! Well, these are the well-known formulas

22:52.640 --> 22:56.200
to generate Pythagorean triples from pairs of integers.

22:56.620 --> 22:59.420
We've derived these formulas, though, you see.

22:59.800 --> 23:02.400
We've derived them from the Gaussian integers.

23:02.400 --> 23:04.740
There are other derivations of these formulas

23:04.740 --> 23:06.740
based on using rational numbers.

23:07.460 --> 23:09.980
I think you can find that, excuse me,

23:11.200 --> 23:14.840
in, um, in Stillwell's book, if I recall correctly.

23:15.360 --> 23:17.260
Uh, let me, let me, let me, let me, let me, let me look.

23:18.420 --> 23:19.120
Do, do, do, do, do.

23:19.160 --> 23:20.420
I think it's in Chapter 1.

23:21.780 --> 23:23.340
Chapter 1 of Stillwell, yeah.

23:24.800 --> 23:25.800
Let's see here.

23:26.440 --> 23:26.800
Eh.

23:29.340 --> 23:31.820
Yeah, so here, in Chapter 1 of Stillwell,

23:32.640 --> 23:34.800
he says, around 300 BCE,

23:35.500 --> 23:37.380
Euclid showed that all natural number solutions

23:37.380 --> 23:39.280
of x squared plus y squared equals to z squared

23:39.280 --> 23:40.600
can be produced by the formulas

23:40.600 --> 23:42.680
u squared minus v squared w,

23:43.600 --> 23:44.500
2u v w,

23:45.080 --> 23:47.240
and u squared plus v squared w, right?

23:47.880 --> 23:51.280
Letting u and v and w run through all the natural numbers, right?

23:51.940 --> 23:53.140
So, ah!

23:53.840 --> 23:56.560
And then Diophantus solved it using rational numbers,

23:56.560 --> 23:58.860
about 200, um, 280.

23:58.860 --> 24:02.940
And, um, yeah, so this is pretty old, right?

24:03.060 --> 24:06.860
But the, um, I assure you that we had to work

24:06.860 --> 24:10.520
not nearly as hard to derive these formulas as they did,

24:10.600 --> 24:13.720
because we have the magic of Gaussian integers.

24:13.720 --> 24:16.700
And, um, so here's an application of those.

24:17.260 --> 24:19.060
We can generate Pythagorean triples

24:19.060 --> 24:23.240
by just selecting any pair of values we wish for u and v.

24:23.800 --> 24:24.380
For example,

24:24.760 --> 24:25.940
if you use 10 and 7,

24:26.760 --> 24:29.620
and you plug it into these formulas that we just derived,

24:30.320 --> 24:32.340
you get 51, 140, and 149,

24:32.340 --> 24:36.320
which gives us that 51 squared plus 140 squared

24:36.320 --> 24:38.640
is equal to 149 squared.

24:39.960 --> 24:42.400
So, that's, that's pretty neat.

24:42.480 --> 24:44.940
Now, listen, there's, um, you know,

